---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import DecklistTooltip from "@components/DecklistTooltip.astro";
import { Card, Alert } from "webcoreui/astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Macro Strategy - Replenish Guide"
    description="Learn the core concepts of playing Replenish in Premodern. Understand how to fight through disruption, identify your role in a matchup, and manage your resources."
>
    <PageHeader
        title="Macro Strategy"
        description="Understanding how Replenish ticks is about more than just knowing what your cards do. It's about recognizing when to play the control game, when to race the combo, and how to fight through hate."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/swords.jpg"
        imageAlt="Swords to Plowshares"
        imageCaption="Swords to Plowshares by Kaja Foglio"
        isCard={true}
    />

    <Card title="Fighting Through Disruption" titleTag="h2">
        <p style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-20); margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
            Replenish is incredibly resilient against single-axis strategies. Pure aggro, land destruction, or a pile of counterspells can usually be picked apart if they only attack you from one angle.
            The real challenge starts when an opponent hits you from two angles at once—like early pressure backed up by <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Wasteland">Wasteland</a> and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Rishadan Port">Rishadan Port</a>, or a fast clock supported by cheap counters.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-20);">
            <strong style="color: var(--w-color-primary-10);">Mulliganing Under Pressure:</strong>
            When you're facing down pressure from two different angles, keeping a slow hand and hoping to cantrip your way out of it on turns one and two is a death sentence. Our dedicated card draw doesn't really come online until turn three. Check out the <a href="/mulligan" style="color: var(--w-color-primary); font-weight: 600; text-decoration: none;">Mulligan Guide</a> for more on what hands to keep.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Identifying Your Role" titleTag="h2">
        <p style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-20); margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
            Replenish is a classic Control-Combo deck. To win a full match, you need to correctly identify your role based on what your opponent is doing:
        </p>
        
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1.5rem;">
            <div>
                <strong style="color: var(--w-color-primary-10); display: block; margin-bottom: 0.25rem; font-size: 1.1rem;">
                    1. The Control Plan (Against Aggro)
                </strong>
                <p style="margin: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-20);">
                    Against fast aggressive decks like Goblins, Sligh, or UG Madness, your goal isn't just to survive forever. You need to disrupt their momentum and control the board <em>just long enough</em> to assemble your combo. Use <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a> and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares">Swords to Plowshares</a> to stall the clock while you sculpt your graveyard for a lethal turn before they can burn you out.
                </p>
            </div>
            
            <div>
                <strong style="color: var(--w-color-primary-10); display: block; margin-bottom: 0.25rem; font-size: 1.1rem;">
                    2. The Combo Plan (Against Control & Fast Combo)
                </strong>
                <p style="margin: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-20);">
                    Against slow control decks or faster combo decks, you have to be proactive. Shift gears, fill your graveyard aggressively in the early turns, and force them to have answers before they can establish full control. Jamming an early <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a> or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a> is often the best line.
                </p>
            </div>
            
            <div>
                <strong style="color: var(--w-color-primary-10); display: block; margin-bottom: 0.25rem; font-size: 1.1rem;">
                    3. The Midrange Pivot (Post-Sideboard)
                </strong>
                <p style="margin: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-20);">
                    In Games 2 and 3, opponents will often dilute their deck with graveyard hate like <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a>. This is when Replenish shines by transforming into a UW Midrange deck. By bringing in cards like <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a> or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a>, you completely ignore their hate and beat them down with evasive creatures while they sit on dead graveyard interaction.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Playing the Long Game" titleTag="h2">
        <p style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-20); margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
            Against heavy counterspell decks, think about the game in terms of inevitability. If the game goes extremely long, and you both draw most of your decks, who wins? Since your threat density (with multiple copies of <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a>, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a>, and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a>) is incredibly high, you are usually favored to win attrition battles.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-20);">
            In these matchups, patience and resource preservation are everything. Don't waste your key spells or discard important combo pieces just for a temporary advantage. Make sure every threat you cast forces them to use a real answer. Eventually, your final spell will resolve simply because they ran out of counters.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Don't Loot Blindly" titleTag="h2">
        <p style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-20); margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
            A common mistake for newer pilots is activating <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a> or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a> repeatedly without a clear plan. 
        </p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 1.25rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-20);">
            Before you activate an engine, you should always know the answers to these two questions:
        </p>
        <ul style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-20); padding-left: 1.5rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.5rem;">
            <li>Which exact cards in your hand are you willing to discard?</li>
            <li>What specific card or answer are you digging for?</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-20);">
            If you don't know the answer to both, <strong style="color: var(--w-color-primary-10);">don't activate it.</strong> Reckless looting against a control deck risks throwing away important lands or redundant combo pieces, leaving you vulnerable in the late game. Always have a purpose for every card you discard, and try to cast your card draw during your opponent's end step whenever possible.
        </p>
    </Card>
    <DecklistTooltip />
</Layout>
